WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.055 --> 00:00:01.680
SPEAKER: Kubeflow is
all about creating

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.080
a portable and scalable
environment for your machine

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:04.940
learning workflows.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.690
Let's take a look at
how to set up and get

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.271
started working with Kubeflow.

00:00:08.271 --> 00:00:11.898
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.898 --> 00:00:14.190
Today, we'll talk about how
to set up Kubeflow and then

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.413
go through a deployment.

00:00:15.413 --> 00:00:17.580
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.670
check out the link below to read
more about setting up Kubeflow.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.620
Now, one of the key
goals of Kubeflow

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.650
is to be portable so you can
run your ML workflow anywhere.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.260
More specifically, it can be
run anywhere Kubernetes can

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.270
be run, which is a
whole lot of places,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.880
like running a Kubernetes
cluster on-prem or a managed

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.870
Kubernetes service in the cloud.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.660
You can use the Kfctl command
line interface to set up

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.150
Kubeflow on your cluster.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.220
Kfctl is flexible so
you can install it

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.750
on a variety of environments.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.900
But it's a command
line tool, and it takes

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.690
some additional configuration.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.780
So let's take a look at
another way to install.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.540
We'll go with the GUI option and
use the Google Cloud deployer.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.100
This quick form
can get you quickly

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.980
set up with Kubeflow after just
filling out some information.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.210
Once we click the Sign
In with Google button,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.730
we'll be prompted
for some access.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.340
Since Kubeflow will be deploying
resources to our Google Cloud

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.188
project.

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:04.980
If you don't want to
use this web deployer,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.200
check out that link below
for other ways to deploy.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.050
Now we can see the specific
information needed to deploy.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.627
The first thing we'll need
is a project to deploy into.

00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:14.460
If you're just playing
around with Kubeflow,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.880
I recommend creating a
new Google Cloud project

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.905
so you have a safe
sandbox to play around in.

00:01:18.905 --> 00:01:20.280
There's a link in
the description

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.863
to help you through
that if you're not

00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:23.680
familiar with creating projects.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.620
In addition, you'll need
to be a project owner

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.960
and make sure this project
has billing enabled,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.490
since a default
deployment currently

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.400
comes with more resources
than the free trial allows.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.100
Don't worry.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.600
You can always
remove the deployment

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.660
when you're done testing
things if you'd like.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.300
The first box is where we'll
put the ID of the Google Cloud

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.520
project where we want
to deploy Kubeflow.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.190
And the second contains the
name for the deployment.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.230
We could change it if
we had multiple clusters

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.820
or other tools in the project.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.320
But the default
name is Kubeflow.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.350
So let's leave that as is.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.890
There is also another
option for how to connect.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.330
You can choose IAP, which
stands for Identity Aware Proxy,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.038
or just choose a
username and password.

00:02:00.038 --> 00:02:01.830
If you're just playing
around and learning,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.070
go ahead and choose Username
and Password to save some time.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.907
Otherwise, you'll
want to use IAP

00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:08.699
for things like your
production environment

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.470
and to protect any
sensitive data.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.840
After that, you can choose
your zone and version.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.080
Or just leave them as is
and click Create deployment.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.917
This could take up
to half an hour,

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:19.750
but you'll see a window
pop up at the bottom

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.380
with a bit more information
about what's going on.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.200
Behind the scenes, Kubeflow
is creating all the resources

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.230
you'll need, including the
Kubernetes cluster and service

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.130
accounts.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.620
Let's switch to a deployment
that we've already created.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.140
After logging in with our
selected connection option,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.013
we'll get the
Kubeflow interface.

00:02:36.013 --> 00:02:37.680
There's a lot of
options here, and we'll

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.763
start to go over the
different components and dive

00:02:39.763 --> 00:02:42.180
into some of them deeper
in our next videos.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.810
Just to make sure
everything's working,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.530
let's quickly launch a
Jupyter notebook interface.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.010
First, choose a namespace
in the top left.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.510
One will be created for
you through the setup.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.610
After that, just click
on Notebook servers

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:54.960
and click New Server.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.110
We can give it a simple name
like Test and then launch it.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.610
It can take a few
minutes to launch,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.220
since it's handling all of the
work of creating the computing

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.870
resources you'll need to run it.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.600
Once the notebook server is up,
we can connect and then create

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.280
a simple Python notebook.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.170
As you can see,
everything is working.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.390
And we haven't had to run
any commands to create

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.293
the necessary resources that
are needed to run the notebook.

00:03:15.293 --> 00:03:17.460
It can be pretty straightforward
to set up Kubeflow,

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.620
and it helps distract
you from the manual steps

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.300
when growing your ML workflows.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.700
Don't forget, you can
go to Deployment Manager

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.430
in the Google Cloud Console to
delete the deployment if you

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.020
want to shut everything down.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.760
Just take a look for
the deployment name

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.540
you specified in the web UI.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.370
The default name
is just Kubeflow.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:34.950
In the next video,
we'll talk more

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.930
about the different
components in Kubeflow.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.420
Check out the links below
to try it out for yourself.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

